course: dictionary
lesson: 14553
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A phenomenon is something that happens or exists which people can see or study. Most often it is something in nature, like a rainbow or an earthquake. «Scientists watched the strange phenomenon in the sky all night.» The word tells you it is something worth looking at and explaining.
explain: The word can also describe a person or thing that is unusual and very impressive. «Maya is a footballing phenomenon; she scored in every game this season.» Here phenomenon means an amazing, one-of-a-kind talent, not a weather event. The clue scored in every game shows it is about being amazing.
explain: Phenomenon can also mean something very popular that spreads everywhere. «The new game became a worldwide phenomenon within a month.» The clues worldwide and within a month show that lots of people everywhere joined in. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence best.
explain: Phenomenon is a noun, and its plural is phenomena. «Scientists study many natural phenomena, such as tides and the Northern Lights.» To check your meaning, swap in a word like thing or event. If the sentence still makes sense, you are on the right track.
fact: A phenomenon is something that happens or exists which can be seen or studied.
fact: The plural of phenomenon is phenomena.
fact: «Phenomenon» can mean a person or thing that is unusual and impressive.
fact: «Phenomenon» can also mean something very popular and widespread.
fact: Rainbows, earthquakes, tides and the Northern Lights are natural phenomena.
exercise: «Scientists watched the strange phenomenon in the sky all night.» What is a phenomenon here? | A cloud that is always grey | A small, noisy machine | *Something that happens in nature that can be studied | A kind of sky-blue paint
exercise: «The new game became a worldwide phenomenon within a month.» What does phenomenon mean here? | A game nobody has heard of | *Something very popular that spread everywhere | A game for one player only | A game that was once broken
exercise: What is the plural of phenomenon? | *phenomena | phenomenal | phenomenas | phenomenons
test: «Maya is a footballing phenomenon; she scored in every game this season.» What does phenomenon mean here? | *An unusual, impressive person | A football match on TV | A player who is bad at football | A kind of football boot
test: Which of these is a natural phenomenon? | A school bell | A bicycle | A cup of tea | *Tides going in and out of the sea
exam: In a science lesson your teacher asks you to explain a phenomenon. What is she asking for? | *An explanation of something that happens in nature | A drawing of a school bell | A story about a famous footballer | A list of your favourite games
